[For "black"]
[Kind "gambit"]
[Start "open-centre-game 2...exd4"]
[Name "The Danish Gambit: 3.c3 d5"]

3. c3 { The Danish Gambit. White offers a second pawn to open lines for the bishops. [%cal Gc3d4] }
3... d5 { Hits back in the centre, and takes a lot less learning than grabbing the pawns. 3...dxc3 is the common move, and the engine likes it a little more, but White scores 58% after it and only 46% after this. [%cal Gd5e4] }
4. exd5 { The most common move. }
( 4. cxd4? { Wins the pawn back, but it leaves e4 to go. }
  4... dxe4 { Black is a pawn up. [%csl Ge4] }
  5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Bc4 Be7 { Black guards everything and castles next, and stays a clean pawn up. } )
( 4. e5? { Pushes past the pawn and shuts the centre, but c3 is then hanging. }
  4... dxc3 { Takes the pawn. }
  5. Nxc3 c6 { Guards d5, and Black is a pawn up with a solid position. } )
4... Qxd5 { Takes back. The queen is safe here for a while, and d4 is still under fire. [%cal Rd5d4] }
5. cxd4 Nc6 { Hits d4 with a piece. [%cal Rc6d4] }
6. Nf3 Bg4 { Pins the knight that guards d4. [%cal Rg4f3,Rg4d1] [%idea pin] }
7. Be2 Bb4+ { The check brings another piece out, and White's reply blocks the bishop's way to d4's guard on c3. }
8. Nc3 Bxf3 { Now takes off the guard of d4. [%idea remove-the-guard] }
9. Bxf3 Qc4 { The queen steps away from the bishop and keeps its eye on d4. The game is level. Do not take on d4 yet: the queen on d1 guards it. [%cal Rc4d4] } *
